# ulmo 0.11.1

This patch release makes main-module selection more convenient in the
command-line driver.

## Infer the main module from its source

For a file `Hello.om` declaring `MODULE Hello;`, use:

```sh
ulmo -m Hello.om
./Hello
```

The wrapper reads the module name from the source header, compiles that
file and links the executable. Additional source files can follow:

```sh
ulmo -m Hello.om o.om
ulmo -m Hello.om -o hello
```

The explicit form remains supported:

```sh
ulmo -m Hello Hello.om
```

A bare `-m` before other options also infers the main module when exactly
one implementation source is given:

```sh
ulmo -m -o hello Hello.om
```

When multiple implementation sources make that form ambiguous, the driver
asks for an explicit main module or main source file.

## Header handling and checks

- Whitespace and nested comments are accepted before and between header
  tokens, including comments containing misleading `MODULE` declarations.
- Only the header is read for inference, keeping memory use small.
- Missing files, invalid headers and definition-only inputs are rejected
  before linking.
- Compilation without `-m` continues to produce object files only.
- Added `make check-cli` for wrapper regression checks.

Command-line checks passed on AMD64 and i386, and the installed wrapper
was verified using a staged Linux installation.

The change is in the shell wrapper; the existing Oberon compiler and
runtime can be used with an updated wrapper. A rewrite of the driver into
Oberon is not required for this shortcut.

Full changes: https://github.com/norayr/ulmo/compare/v0.11.0...v0.11.1
